    "updated_at": "2023-09-06T08:59:01Z",
    "body": "I think it's undisputed that the decision is in your hands, however I'd like you to consider that:\r\n\r\n1. there are many people who implement some part of the HTML specification and would benefit from being able to easily track the changes of that specific part\r\n2. there are many people who are intimidated by such a long file and will struggle with editing it because they're not used to it\r\n\r\nWhen it comes to these two concerns, I think the first one weighs much more heavily. HTML is a \"living standard\" ... how are implementors supposed to keep track of changes to some part of the spec?\r\n\r\nBack in 2018 [you elaborated](https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/3705#issuecomment-391345509):\r\n\r\n> It's supremely valuable to be able to edit the document as a single file, and use text editor features such as go-to-line, search, highlight all instances, etc. that rely on it being a single file.\r\n\r\nI'd argue go-to-line isn't really necessary when the file isn't hundred thousand lines long. When it comes to searching multiple files I think `git grep` would work great if all the source files would reside in the `src` directory. You could just do `git grep <term> src`.\r\n\r\n> The single-file workflow is also what every other spec I work with does.\r\n\r\nDoes any of these other spec files also have over hundred thousand lines, while covering different topics (i.e. it's reasonable that you'd only  want to implement part of it)? I'd like to point out that there are certainly modern specifications that split up their contents across files e.g. the [matrix-spec](https://github.com/matrix-org/matrix-spec/tree/main/content), [ircv3](https://github.com/ircv3/ircv3-specifications/tree/master/extensions) (they even use a Markdown rather than a proprietary markup language that looks like HTML but isn't HTML ... but that's a different issue).\r\n\r\nRegarding your final note that page names change ... this isn't an issue at all ... files can be renamed and git detects file renames (so the history gets carried over).",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
